[542.19758, "o", "nes \u001b[38;5;246m(ctrl+r to expand)\u001b[39m\u001b(B\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;114m●\u001b[39m \u001b[1mRead\u001b[22m(packages/mcp-server/src/api-client/schema.ts)\r\n  ⎿  Read \u001b[1m55\u001b[22m lines \u001b[38;5;246m(ctrl+r to expand)\u001b[39m\u001b(B\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m Now I understand the issue. The \u001b[38;5;153mTraceSchema\u001b[39m expects an array of \u001b[38;5;153mTraceSpanSchema\u001b[39m objects, but according to the error\r\n  analysis, the Sentry API can return a mixed array containing both span objects AND issue objects. Let me check how\r\n  this is used and create a fix:\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m Let me check the docs for guidance on making API schema changes:\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;114m●\u001b[39m \u001b[1mRead\u001b[22m(docs/api-patterns.mdc)\r\n  ⎿  Read \u001b[1m220\u001b[22m lines \u001b[38;5;246m(ctrl+r to expand)\u001b[39m\u001b(B\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m Now let me look at the actual Sentry API response format to understand what we're dealing with. Let me check the test\r\n  fixtures or examples:\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;114m●\u001b[39m \u001b[1mSearch\u001b[22m(pattern: \"SerializedIssue|SerializedSpan\", path: \"packages\")\r\n  ⎿  Found \u001b[1m0 \u001b[22mfiles \u001b(B\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m Let me check the test files for the trace functionality:\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;114m●\u001b[39m \u001b[1mSearch\u001b[22m(pattern: \"getTrace|TraceSchema\", path: \"packages/mcp-server/src\", glob: \"*.test.ts\")\r\n  ⎿  Found \u001b[1m3 \u001b[22mfiles \u001b[38;5;246m(ctrl+r to expand)\u001b[39m\u001b(B\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;114m●\u001b[39m \u001b[1mRead\u001b[22m(packages/mcp-server/src/tools/get-trace-details.test.ts)\r\n  ⎿  Read \u001b[1m294\u001b[22m lines \u001b[38;5;246m(ctrl+r to expand)\u001b[39m\u001b(B\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m Let me look at the trace fixture to understand the actual data structure:\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;114m●\u001b[39m \u001b[1mSearch\u001b[22m(pattern: \"traceFixture\", path: \"packages/mcp-server-mocks\")\r\n  ⎿  Found \u001b[1m1 \u001b[22mfile \u001b[38;5;246m(ctrl+r to expand)\u001b[39m\u001b(B\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;114m●\u001b[39m \u001b[1mRead\u001b[22m(packages/mcp-server-mocks/src/index.ts)\r\n  ⎿  Read \u001b[1m1357\u001b[22m lines \u001b[38;5;246m(ctrl+r to expand)\u001b[39m\u001b(B\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m Now let me look at the actual trace fixture to understand the data structure:\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;114m●\u001b[39m \u001b[1mRead\u001b[22m(packages/mcp-server-mocks/src/fixtures/trace.json)\r\n  ⎿  Read \u001b[1m50\u001b[22m lines \u001b[38;5;246m(ctrl+r to expand)\u001b[39m\u001b(B\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m I can see the fixture shows a standard span structure. Based on the error analysis, the issue is that the Sentry API\r\n  can return a mixed array containing both span objects (like in the fixture) AND issue objects that don't have all the\r\n  required span fields. Let me update the todos and implement the fix:\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;231m●\u001b[39m Now I'll implement the fix. The problem is that \u001b[38;5;153mTraceSchema\u001b[39m expects all items to be \u001b[38;5;153mTraceSpanSchema\u001b[39m objects, but the\r\n  API can return a mix of spans and issues. I need to create a more flexible schema that can handle both types:\r\n\r\n\u001b[38;5;114m●\u001b[39m \u001b[1mUpdate\u001b[22m(packages/mcp-server/src/api-client/schema.ts)\r\n  ⎿  Updated \u001b[1mpackages/mcp-server/src/api-client/schema.ts\u001b[22m with \u001b[1m24\u001b[22m additions and \u001b[1m3\u001b[22m removals\u001b(B\r\n     \u001b[38;5;246m  614 \u001b[38;5;231m   );\u001b[39m\r\n     \u001b[38;5;246m  615 \u001b[38;5;231m   \u001b[39m\r\n     \u001b[38;5;246m  616 \u001b[38;5;231m   /**\u001b[39m\r\n     \u001b[38;5;246m  617 \u001b[48;5;65m\u001b[38;5;231m+   * Schema for issue objects that can appear in trace responses.\u001b[39m\u001b[49m\r\n     \u001b[38;5;246m  618 \u001b[48;5;65m\u001b[38;5;231m+   *\u001b[39m\u0